  "worked_solution": "If $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,\u2026 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.3),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3), & x=1,2,3\u2026 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{0}(0.3)+(0.7)^{1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2}(0.3)+(0.7)^{3}(0.3)+(0.7)^{4}(0.3) \\\\  & \u22480.8319.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>5) & =1-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & \u22481-0.8319 \\\\  & =0.1681.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
